# How many edge-function tests the suite is known to run.
#
# ONE definition, read by both runners: the "Count the edge tests that
# ran" step in `.github/workflows/ci.yml` and
# `supabase/functions/_local_check/check_locally.sh`.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. `deno test` over a file that defines no tests prints
#
#     ok | 0 passed | 0 failed
#
# and exits 0. Verified on deno 2.9.6: a passing test exits 0, a failing
# test exits 1, and a file with NOTHING IN IT exits 0 as well. So a
# `Deno.test` block that stopped registering -- deleted, commented out,
# or left inside a condition that is never true -- takes its assertions
# with it and CI stays green. There are 37 test files run as 37 separate
# steps, and until this file existed not one of them counted anything.
#
# This is the same hole the SQL suite had, and `supabase/tests/assertion_floor`
# is the same answer to it. The difference is only in what gets counted:
# there it is `NOTICE:  ok ` lines, here it is the `N passed` that deno
# prints itself.
#
# WHAT THE NUMBER IS. The total `passed` over every test file ci.yml
# runs, in one combined invocation. 467 today, which is also exactly the
# number of `Deno.test(` blocks on disk -- so every block registers and
# runs, and the two counts agreeing is itself worth knowing.
#
# HOW TO CHANGE IT. Raise it when the number goes up, which is ordinary:
# a new test is a new count. Lower it only on purpose, in a commit that
# says why. A DIP with every step still green is the thing to
# investigate, not to paper over.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER. The 37 individual steps remain authoritative:
# each runs its own file with its own permission flags, and the flags are
# the point -- `scheduler_test.ts` is run with none at all, and a day
# when it needs one is a day worth noticing. The counting pass uses the
# UNION of the flags, so it is deliberately weaker on permissions and
# says nothing about them. It counts; it does not replace.
#
# The bare integer below is the value. Anything else here is a comment;
# both readers anchor on `^[0-9]+$`.
503
